<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yerevan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Erevan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yerevan is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, on the Hrazdan river within sight of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-ararat">Mount Ararat</a>. Its history begins with the Urartian fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erebuni">Erebuni</a>, founded in 782 BCE. Known as Erivan, it was the seat of an Iranian province and later of the Erivan Khanate until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> captured it in 1827. It became the capital of the first Armenian republic in 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yerevan.jpg" alt="Yerevan" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Սէրուժ Ուրիշեան (Serouj Ourishian) · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Ararat_and_the_Yerevan_skyline_(June_2018).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Yerevan lies on the banks of the Hrazdan River, in the north-east of the Ararat Plain, in the central-western part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and the heart of the Armenian Highland. Its average height is 990 metres. The upper part of the city is surrounded by mountains on three sides, and the Hrazdan divides it in two through a canyon. Mount Ararat, visible from any part of the city, is its principal symbol.</p><p>The origin of the name is unknown. One theory ties it to the Armenian king Yervand (Orontes) IV, but it more likely derives from the Urartian fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erebuni">Erebuni</a>, founded in 782 BCE, and the name evolved through Erevani and Erevan into Yerevan as Urartian and Armenian elements blended.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The territory has been inhabited since approximately the second half of the 4th millennium BCE. The southern district of Shengavit was populated from at least 3200 BCE in the period of the Kura–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araxes</a> culture of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, and excavations there found a series of large round buildings and ritual installations. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argishti-i">Argishti I</a> of Urartu founded the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erebuni">Erebuni</a> in 782 BCE at the western extreme of the Ararat Plain, as a great administrative and religious centre. By the late ancient Armenian Kingdom new capital cities were founded and Yerevan declined.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> forcibly deported hundreds of thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in the Great Surgun of 1603–05, which mostly depopulated the city. An earthquake mostly destroyed it in 1679, and it was rebuilt on a smaller scale. It later became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> it became the capital of the First Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> in 1918, as thousands of survivors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenian-genocide">Armenian genocide</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> arrived. It grew rapidly in the 20th century within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Yerevan is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and one of the world's oldest continuously inhabited cities, with 1,086,677 people. It has been the capital since 1918, the fourteenth in Armenia's history. It is the seat of the Araratian Pontifical Diocese, the largest diocese of the Armenian Apostolic Church.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erebuni">Erebuni Fortress</a> is considered the birthplace of the city, and Saint Gregory Cathedral is the largest Armenian cathedral in the world. Tsitsernakaberd is the official memorial to the victims of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenian-genocide">Armenian genocide</a>. The Matenadaran holds one of the largest depositories of ancient books and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> in the world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yerevan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A cuneiform inscription found in <b>Yerevan</b> records that the city was founded in the summer of 782 BCE by King Argishti I of Urartu, making the capital of the republic one of the world's oldest continuously inhabited cities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenia">Armenia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Yerevan</b> government sent Alexander Khatisian to negotiate with the Turks and appointed a team under General Drastamat Kanayan to hand power to the Bolsheviks.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Yerevan</b>, capital of modern Armenia, traces its origin to the Urartian fortress of Erebuni, founded by Argishti I in 782 BCE, and celebrated its 2,750th anniversary in 1968.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-urartu">Kingdom of Urartu · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>A second war in 1826–1828, despite early successes, ended in defeat and the Treaty of Turkmenchay, which surrendered <b>Erivan</b> and Nakhichevan and fixed the frontier on the Aras river.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Western Armenian took shape in Istanbul, where migrants from across the homeland brought their dialects together; Eastern Armenian, centred in Tbilisi, drew on the dialect of <b>Yerevan</b> and Mount Ararat.</blockquote><a href="/language/armenian">Armenian · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>An earthquake of magnitude about 6 struck around midnight and destroyed Dvin, the large commercial city that was the capital of early medieval Armenia, south of modern <b>Yerevan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-28">28 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
